  "prediction": "**Summary of the Department of Defense (DOD) Workforce Report**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has undergone significant changes in its workforce composition and size since 2001. The DOD's military, civilian, and contractor workforces peaked around 2011 and have since decreased in size. The report highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in managing its workforce, including varying levels of U.S. involvement in conflicts, military to civilian and contractor conversions, and the growth of certain workforces such as acquisition and cyber.\n\nThe report also examines the DOD's management of its workforce, governed by several workforce management statutes, including sections 129, 129a, and 2463 of Title 10 of the United States Code. The DOD has taken steps to better understand the costs associated with its workforces, including improving its methodology for estimating and comparing the full cost of work performed by military and civilian personnel and contractor support.\n\nThe report assesses the DOD's Cost-Comparison Report, which aimed to compare the costs of federal civilian and service contractor personnel performing similar functions at selected installations. The report found that the DOD partially addressed the reporting element to account for the full cost of civilian and contractor personnel by excluding certain non-labor costs from its cost calculations. The report also found that the DOD did not include direct and indirect non-labor costs, such as facility overhead, in its cost comparisons, as required by DOD Instruction 7041.04.\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to the DOD, including:\n\n1. Assess the advantages and disadvantages of allowing the continued use of different cost-estimation tools across the department or directing department-wide application of one tool.\n2. Revise its guidance in accordance with the findings of its assessment.\n3. Include non-labor costs in its cost calculations to account for the full cost of federal civilians and service contractors.\n4. Improve its methodology for estimating and comparing the full cost of work performed by military and civilian personnel and contractor support.\n5. Develop a comprehensive plan to address the challenges associated with the various taxonomies and lexicons associated with articulating the size and composition of federal civilian, military, and contracted services workforces.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for the DOD to improve its workforce management and cost estimation methodologies to better support its mission and make informed decisions about its workforce.",
